{"entityMap":{},"blocks":[{"key":"5bjco","text":"I attended the church of jj","type":"header-one","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"eil4d","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"boq2q","text":"This post will not be a full blown tutorial on `jj` - several exist and would be much better for those wanting to start from scratch. It will be a recollection of the experience I had that solved major problems for me (and made changing my workflow worthwhile). Before the story I want to start with a note on motivations.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8kur2","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"42lqt","text":"Motivations","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"fv91h","text":"Approaching jj face first into the cliff wall of documentation and aforementioned tutorials was entirely un-motivating to me. I know my way around git well enough to navigate complex changes and collaborate quickly. I want to start by validating the principle of not jumping on the new and shiny thing because everyone says it's shiny. Instead, using judgement and discernment around your tools is a very good thing.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"3j0ae","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1oh7n","text":"However,","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4m1j4","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4ktoa","text":"I jumped into a project that needed several improvements to happen in parallel. I wanted to make several small PRs because they were small changes that would have large consequential effects if they were not reviewed properly or glossed over.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"br0j1","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8g324","text":"Then I found myself in a precarious (and perhaps familiar) position: I made a commit and realized I needed to go back and make that change near the beginning of my stack of branches.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8b3k","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8hti4","text":"Aside: personal context","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"a2ime","text":"Another blocker for me adopting jj was how some concepts were synonymous to the git equivalent, some replaced, some evolved, and so on. When learning git, I felt there was too much repetitive typing, so it helped me to learn the sequence of ohmyzsh git plugin aliases to ","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}}]}